WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.087
<v Examiner>Two marks.

2
00:00:02.087 --> 00:00:06.422
<v Examiner>Medicines have a chemical name, a generic name and a brand name.

3
00:00:06.422 --> 00:00:09.943
<v Examiner>Which is most important for the R N to use, and why?

4
00:00:10.393 --> 00:00:11.482
The generic name.

5
00:00:11.482 --> 00:00:17.180
Her model answer: generic names are internationally approved and used by both prescribers

6
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.420
and the people giving the medicine.

7
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:22.109
They reduce the risk of medication errors,

8
00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:27.103
and they let pharmacists dispense the most cost-effective option for families.

9
00:00:27.103 --> 00:00:31.841
Her example of the danger: Panadol and Panadeine both contain paracetamol,

10
00:00:31.841 --> 00:00:35.298
so someone who knows only brand names can double-dose.

11
00:00:35.748 --> 00:00:36.355
<v Examiner>Two marks.

12
00:00:36.355 --> 00:00:41.273
<v Examiner>Choose one legal classification of medicines, and describe how a patient gets it.

13
00:00:41.723 --> 00:00:44.333
Any one, from the Medicines Act 1981.

14
00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:49.835
Prescription medicine: only with a prescription from an authorised prescriber,

15
00:00:49.835 --> 00:00:53.291
like a doctor, a nurse practitioner or a midwife.

16
00:00:53.291 --> 00:00:58.581
Restricted, or pharmacist-only: only from a pharmacist, after their advice,

17
00:00:58.581 --> 00:01:00.062
and a record is kept.

18
00:01:00.062 --> 00:01:02.460
Pharmacy-only: only in a pharmacy.

19
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.692
General sale: any shop, like paracetamol in the supermarket.

20
00:01:07.142 --> 00:01:11.820
Controlled drugs are separate, under the Misuse of Drugs Act 1975.

21
00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:17.773
Class A is the highest risk and is never prescribed, like heroin or methamphetamine.

22
00:01:17.773 --> 00:01:22.025
Class B is high risk but some are prescribed, like morphine.

23
00:01:22.025 --> 00:01:25.498
Class C is lower risk, like codeine and diazepam.

24
00:01:25.498 --> 00:01:31.239
And who's who: Medsafe assesses and monitors the quality and safety of medicines.

25
00:01:31.239 --> 00:01:34.287
Pharmac decides which medicines are funded.

26
00:01:34.737 --> 00:01:40.190
Use the generic name: internationally approved, prevents errors like double-dosing,

27
00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:42.226
and allows the cheapest option.

28
00:01:42.226 --> 00:01:48.008
Legal classes: prescription, restricted or pharmacist-only, pharmacy-only, general sale.

29
00:01:48.008 --> 00:01:53.001
Controlled drugs: Class A never prescribed, B like morphine, C like codeine.

30
00:01:53.001 --> 00:01:54.446
Medsafe checks safety.

31
00:01:54.446 --> 00:01:55.366
Pharmac funds.
